/**
 * School and sports physicals — the paperwork, the coding, and what the chart can reuse.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS
 * Two reasons, and the second is the real one.
 *
 * 1. It targets the JOB, not the tool. Search volume for "AI form filler" will read as
 *    zero because the category does not exist yet — nobody searched "ambient AI scribe"
 *    before scribes existed either. What pediatricians search is the task: school
 *    physical forms, sports physical requirements, whether a physical can be billed
 *    alongside a well visit. This is the same play as
 *    /clinical-documentation-automation/, which went from nothing to position 4–15 in
 *    three weeks by being an honest reference rather than a pitch.
 *
 * 2. It is the landing place for form filling when that ships. The FAQ currently lists
 *    form completion under `limits` — "not yet, in development". When it ships, this
 *    page is already ranking and the "where Stream fits" section changes; the rest
 *    stands on its own either way. Do not pre-announce it here as available.
 *
 * SCOPE: school and sports physicals only. Prior authorisation is a crowded, well-funded
 * category (Innovaccer, Prosper, InsightHealth and others); school forms are barely
 * served. Adding prior auth here would put us in a fight we have no reason to pick.
 *
 * NO AMA DESCRIPTORS ARE REPRODUCED, same rule as the pediatric coding guide: code
 * numbers are ours to reference, the official long descriptors are AMA copyright and
 * licensed separately from product use.
 *
 * ACCURACY BAR: this makes coding and clinical claims to pediatricians. Everything here
 * traces to AAP or AMA guidance, and where practice genuinely varies — payer coverage of
 * sports physicals, state form requirements — the page says it varies rather than
 * picking an answer. A physician should read this before any material change.
 */
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';

/* Rendered below AND declared in FAQPage schema, from this one array. Google treats
   declaring more questions than the page shows as invalid. */
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can you bill a sports physical and a well-child visit at the same appointment?',
    a: 'Often yes, but it depends on the payer and on what you actually did. The preventive visit is the billable service; the sports clearance form is paperwork attached to it. Some payers cover a separate pre-participation evaluation, many do not and the family pays cash. Where a distinct problem is addressed at the same visit, that is a separate matter and is covered in our pediatric visit coding guide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a sports physical count as a well-child visit?',
    a: 'Not automatically. A pre-participation evaluation is narrower than a Bright Futures well visit — it is focused on cardiovascular risk, musculoskeletal history and clearance to play. If you perform a full preventive visit and complete the sports form from it, you did a well visit with paperwork attached. If you only did the clearance exam, you did not do a well visit, and the well visit is still due.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much of a school physical form can come from the existing chart?',
    a: 'More than most practices use. Immunisation history, growth measurements, chronic conditions, current medications and allergies are already documented — the limiting factor is that they are scattered across visits, the state registry and the growth chart rather than being in one place. What genuinely requires today is the physical examination, the clearance decision and the signature.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do school physical forms differ by state?',
    a: 'Yes, substantially. Many states publish a mandatory form; others let districts set their own. Sports clearance often uses a separate form again, frequently based on the AAP and AMSSM pre-participation evaluation monograph. Check your state health department and athletic association rather than assuming last year’s form is current.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can Stream fill in school physical forms?',
    a: 'Not today. Stream keeps the underlying information in one place — immunisations, growth, problems and medications organised per problem rather than scattered across dated notes — which is the part that makes form completion slow. Filling structured forms is in development and not available yet, and we would rather say so than imply otherwise.',
  },
];
