<section class="sp">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="sp-wrap">

      <span class="section-label">Guide</span>
      <h1>School and sports physicals: <em>the paperwork</em></h1>
      <p class="sp-lede">The exam takes ten minutes. The form takes longer, and not because it is difficult — because the answers are scattered across four places and none of them is the form.</p>
      <p class="sp-meta">Written for pediatric practices · Physician-authored · Educational, not compliance advice</p>

      <h2>Why the form is slower than the visit</h2>
      <p>Almost nothing a school or sports form asks for is unknown to the practice. The problem is where the answers live.</p>

      <table class="sp-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>What the form asks</th><th>Where the answer actually is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Immunisation record</td><td>The state registry, and sometimes a different one if the family moved</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Height, weight, BMI percentile</td><td>Today's vitals, plus the growth chart for trajectory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chronic conditions</td><td>Spread across whichever visits they came up in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Current medications and allergies</td><td>The med list, if it has been reconciled recently</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Prior injury, concussion, cardiac history</td><td>A note from two years ago that someone has to find</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vision and hearing screening</td><td>A previous well visit, if it was done there</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Exam findings and clearance</td><td>Today, and only today</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Six of those seven are retrieval. One is clinical work. That ratio is why the paperwork outlasts the appointment, and why it lands in the evening — the exam is done in the room, and the form is finished later when someone has time to go looking.</p>

      <h2>The coding question</h2>
      <p>This is where practices lose money quietly, and where the answer genuinely depends on what happened.</p>

      <h3>A preventive visit with a form attached</h3>
      <p>If you performed a full preventive visit and completed the sports form from it, the billable service is the preventive visit — coded by age and by whether the patient is new or established. The form is paperwork arising from it, not a separate service. Age bands and the codes are in our <a href="/guides/pediatric-visit-coding/">pediatric visit coding guide</a>.</p>

      <h3>A clearance exam on its own</h3>
      <p>A pre-participation evaluation is narrower than a Bright Futures well visit — focused on cardiovascular risk, musculoskeletal history and fitness to play. Coverage varies considerably: some payers reimburse a separate pre-participation evaluation, many do not, and a cash fee is common. <strong>Check your own payer mix rather than assuming.</strong></p>

      <div class="sp-note">
        <p><strong>The part that is most often missed:</strong> a clearance exam is not a well visit. If a teenager comes in for sports clearance in August and that is all that happens, the well visit is still due — and in most practices nothing will surface that fact until the following year. That is a documentation and recall problem rather than a coding one.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>When a real problem comes up during the visit</h3>
      <p>Asthma that needs a changed plan before the season, a knee that has not recovered, a new murmur. That is a separately identifiable problem addressed alongside a preventive service, and it is coded as such — the same scenario the <a href="/guides/pediatric-visit-coding/">coding guide</a> covers in detail, with the documentation standard set out in <a href="/guides/the-defensible-visit/">The Defensible Visit</a>.</p>

      <h2>State and sport variation</h2>
      <p>There is no single national form. Many states publish a mandatory school health form; others leave it to districts. Sports clearance is frequently a separate form again, commonly derived from the AAP and AMSSM pre-participation evaluation monograph, which is the reference most state athletic associations build on.</p>
      <p>Two practical consequences: the form you used last August may have been revised, and a family with children in two districts may hand you two different forms for the same information.</p>

      <h2>What can be reused, and what cannot</h2>
      <div class="sp-box">
        <h3>Safe to carry forward</h3>
        <p>Immunisation history from the state registry, growth measurements you recorded, documented chronic conditions, prior injuries noted in your own charts. These are facts your practice either recorded or retrieved from an authoritative source.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sp-box">
        <h3>Verify before it goes on a form</h3>
        <p>Anything taken from an outside document — a referral letter, records from a previous practice, a parent's recollection. An outside medication list can be months stale and an allergy can be missing entirely. Knowing <em>where</em> a fact came from is not the same as knowing it is <em>true</em>, and a form leaves the building with a signature on it.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <div class="sp-faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="sp-fit">
        <h2>Where Stream fits</h2>
        <p>Stream does not fill in forms. That is in development and not available today, and we would rather say so here than let you find out on a call.</p>
        <p>What it does do is remove the retrieval. Every visit is organised by medical problem rather than by date, so the asthma history is one thread instead of six notes to read in order, and inbound documents — outside records, specialist letters, prior immunisation records — are read and filed under the problems they concern, each extracted fact linked back to the page it came from.</p>
        <p>For well-visit recall specifically, <a href="/for/pediatrics/">Stream for pediatrics</a> sweeps the whole active panel against the Bright Futures schedule nightly, which is what surfaces the teenager who came in for sports clearance in August and never had the well visit.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="sp-disclaim">This guide is educational and reflects general AAP, AMA and AMSSM guidance as of October 2026. It is not coding, billing or compliance advice. Payer policy, state requirements and form versions change — verify against your own payers, your state health department and your state athletic association before relying on any of it.</p>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Page>
